    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes. Here's what I did.\n\n## Change\n\nThe `build` method in `CustomerCodes.java` already received the `existing` collection but ignored it. I made it de-duplicate the computed code:\n\n```java\nstatic String build(Owner owner, Collection<Owner> existing) {\n    return deduplicate(region(owner) + \"-\" + hash8(owner.getTelephone() + owner.getLastName()), existing);\n}\n\n/** {@code code}, or the first {@code code-<n>} (n starting at 2) not held by an owner in {@code existing}. */\nprivate static String deduplicate(String code, Collection<Owner> existing) {\n    Set<String> used = new HashSet<>();\n    for (Owner owner : existing) {\n        used.add(owner.getCustomerCode());\n    }\n    String candidate = code;\n    for (int n = 2; used.contains(candidate); n++) {\n        candidate = code + \"-\" + n;\n    }\n    return candidate;\n}\n```\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\n- **One file touched** (`CustomerCodes.java`) \u2014 no new class, so the file-count multiplier stays at 1.\n- **`build` changed by a single line** \u2014 it still has cyclomatic complexity 1; only the return expression was wrapped in a call.\n- **New behaviour lives in a small, cohesive helper** (`deduplicate`) rather than growing `build` or bloating a large class. `CustomerCodes` is a tiny utility class, so `WMC_other` for both changed functions is small.\n\nThe rule is satisfied: the first free `-<n>` (smallest n \u2265 2) is appended on collision, and the de-duplicated `customerCode` is returned. All tests are green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
